          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            One may interpret mathematical
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            as being a formal language for talking about the collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
            into a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            of a given
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+subobjects"} poset of subobjects
            of that object.
          %p
            More generally, one may interpret
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            and notably
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            as being a formal language for talking about
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+categories"} slice categories
          %p
            Conversely, starting with a given theory of logic or a given type theory, we say that it has a
            %em categorical semantics
            if there is a category such that the given theory is that of its slice categories, if it is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of that category.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            For the general idea, for the moment see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            the section
            %em
              %a{:href => "type+theory#CategoricalSemantics"} An introduction for category theorists
            and see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
          %h3#of_dependent_type_theory Of dependent type theory
          %p
            We discuss how to interpret judgements of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            in a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+model+of+dependent+types"} categorical model of dependent types
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi cod
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cod : \mathcal{C}^I \to \mathcal{C}
            for its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain+fibration"} codomain fibration
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\chi : \mathcal{C}^{op} \to Cat
          %p
            for the corresponding
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+construction"} classifying functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+category"} indexing
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                  %mo :
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\chi : \Gamma \mapsto \mathcal{C}_{/\Gamma}
          %p
            that sends an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
            over it, and sends a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \Gamma \to \Gamma'
            to the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi Γ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f^\ast : \mathcal{C}_{/\Gamma'}
                  \\to
                  \\mathcal{C}_{/\Gamma}
                  \\,.
          %p
            We give now rules for choices “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [x y z]
            ” that associate with every string “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x y z
            ” of symbols in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            objects and morphisms in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %em an interpretation
            \/ a choice of
            %em categorical semantics
            of the type theory in the category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %h4#ContextsAndTypeJudgements Contexts and type judgements
          %ol
