          %h3#of_homotopy_type_theory Of homotopy type theory
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics+of+homotopy+type+theory"} categorical semantics of homotopy type theory
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models+in+presheaf+toposes"} models in presheaf toposes
          %h2#Terminology Terminology
          %p
            Most usage in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            of the adjective “categorical” in relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is a shorthand, and arguably an unfortunate one, for “category theoretic”, i.e. for “as seen through the lens of, hence as treated with the concepts and tools of category theory”.
          %p
            Compare to “numerical” versus “number theoretic”: The interest of number theory is not in numerical methods! Numerical statements are made by engineers. For category theorists it’s worse: Their profession is not to do
            = succeed "," do
              %em categorical arguments
          %p
            While the careful dictionaries list a secondary sense of “categorical”, as “relating to a category”, they hardly have the categories of Kant in mind here, much less those of Eilenberg &amp; MacLane; instead they refer to
            = succeed ":" do
              %em categorization
            %a{:href => "https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/categorical"} offers
            “categorical systems for classifying books” as an example for what “categorical” could refer to, in this secondary sense, in common language. Therefore it does not seem to help much that this secondary common sense of “categorical” is
            %a{:href => "https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/categorial"} offered
            as the primary sense of “categorial” (without the second “c”!). But this is probably the rationale behind a more widespread use of “categorial semantics” over “categorical semantics” in the field of formal logic.
          %p
            All this notwithstanding, the use in mathematics of “categorical”, as in
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            is wide-spread, even standard. Since unfortunate choice of terminology in mathematics is rather common (compare “perverse schobers”!), and since the primary purpose of mathematical terminology is communication rather than, yes, proper categorization, there may not be much gain in opposing this trend, and not much success to doing so by replacing unfortunate terminology with something that looks like the same unfortunate terminology but with a typo in it.
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+model+of+dependent+types"} categorical model of dependent types
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initiality+conjecture"} initiality conjecture
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Awodey%27s+conjecture"} Awodey's conjecture
          %h2#references References
          %p A standard textbook reference for categorical semantics of logic is section D1.2 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
          %p
            The categorical semantics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+categories"} locally cartesian closed categories
            is essentially due to
          %ul
            %li#Seely
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+A.+G.+Seely"}> R. A. G. Seely
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Locally cartesian closed categories and type theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.mcgill.ca/rags/LCCC/LCCC.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            For more references on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+category+theory+and+type+theory"} relation between category theory and type theory
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li#Pavlovic1990
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Du%C5%A1ko+Pavlovi%C4%87"}> Duško Pavlović
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Predicates and Fibrations
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://dusko.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/1990-proefschrift-dusko.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Pavlovic-PredicatesAndFibrations.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p Lecture notes on this include for instance.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hofmann"}> Martin Hofmann
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Syntax and semantics of dependent types
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.36.8985"} web
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roy+L.+Crole"}> Roy L. Crole
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categories for types
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139172707"} doi:10.1017/CBO9781139172707
          %p See also section B.3 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Warren"}> Michael Warren
              ,
              %em Homotopy Theoretic Aspects of Constructive Type Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/awodey/students/warren.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            A comprehensive definition of semantics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type-theoretic+model+categories"} type-theoretic model categories
            is in section 2 of
          %ul
            %li#Shulman12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Shulman"}> Michael Shulman
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Univalence for inverse diagrams and homotopy canonicity
              %em From type theory and homotopy theory to Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
              ) June 2015 (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1203.3253"}> arXiv:1203.3253
